A||N||8||Roy||4.3l v-6 conversion||||1||
Z||000000||Roy||01-26-2000||12:26 PM||||A few months ago, my 89 s-10 blazer got rear-ended by a ups truck. Not the puny ones <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> The big ones. Well, it was considered totaled by the insurance company. I had just rebuilt the 4.3 for it when this happend. It has maybe 1.5k miles on. I bought the truck back from the insurance company with a salavage title. Ive pulled the engine. And sent the rest of the truck back the junk yard <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Im wondering if anyone here has done a 4.3 conversion. I have this wonderful engine, and the more I think about it, it would be great in the fiero. Its like 160hp stock and 240lbs-ft of torque, I think. How much do you think I'd spend putting this engine into a 84 or a 86. Also can a 5spd getrag bolt on to this engine?<P>Roy||||reg||1
Z||000001||DRH||01-26-2000||01:25 PM||||I believe the 4.3 is a "chopped off" 5.7 V8. That should mean you could bolt to the trans with a V8 kit???<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tom88gt||01-26-2000||01:42 PM||||don't waste your time or $.  The 4.3 is basically a shorted v-8.  So you have the same width problems (90' v-6) and same costs for installation.  A v-8 will run smoother than any v-6 (except 120' v-6).  Thus will be easier on your transaxle.  The weight difference is probably negligable.  Plus parts will be cheaper than the 4.3<P>The only v-6 I would recommend is another 60' Chevy.  There are some other great v-6s but I think they are also 90' motors, so the width problem is the same as a v-8.  Just not as smooth and rougher at rpm.<P>Get a book called V-6 PERFORMANCE (covers ford, chevy, buick).  Lots of good back ground on v-6s.  Will tell you all sorts of stuff about v-6 that the engine swap companies don't.<P>Get all the info you can and decide whats right for you.  Because I won't do it doesn't mean its not right for you. ||||reg||12
Z||000003||william_thorniii||01-26-2000||01:43 PM||||DRH is right... the 4.3L is a 350 less two cylendars.  A standard V-8 mounting kit should have everything that you need. <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||01-26-2000||01:49 PM||||We had a member of our club put in a 4.3 V6 into his Fiero. It was a very.....unothodox installation as he did it himself. He fabricated his own steel adapter plate and went to town on the car. He was obsessed with weight saving. The hood was reaplaced with sheet aluminum, the guts of the doors were taken out, even the windows, the interior was gone, he made it into the first chop top Fiero. It was a mean looking SOB. He had NOS on the carbed engine. Even after he hydroplaned it into the center divider of the freeway he still worked it. The blow that finally got him was when he burned a piston. It now resides in the FOCOA museum. <P>Since the 4.3 is a 90 degree V6 it takes up a lot more room. This guy simply cut a hole in the firewall so he could sit in the passenger seat to change his back plug wires! Like I said he did his Fiero his way. That is the only 4.3 that I know of.||||reg||1
Z||000005||squid||01-27-2000||06:16 PM||||You wont need a complete v8 kit from Archie. You also wont need an electric water pump or custom water pump drive setup, the special balancer/pulley, or Archie's alternator bracket because your engine is about 4 inches shorter than a v8 sbc. Just about everything will swap with a smallblock chevy (within reason). The oil filter base is different from an sbc in that its smaller and metric. Archies prices have gone up but it will probably cost about $1500 since you already have a good, complete engine/donor. Width is not a problem with the 6 but with the 8 the hinge box would require some trimming to clear the rocker which extends farther to the right.The passengerside engine support would require some custom work since Archies kit is for a v8.The lesser torque of the 6 versus the 8 will be easier on your driveline too not that its a problem according to Archie. Keeping the 6 cool is easier than with the 8.The bolt pattern for all fiero transmissions are the same.Quad-4's bellhousing is different. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||william_thorniii||01-27-2000||07:09 PM||||Tom,<P>You might want to rethink your statement about parts for the 4.3L being more expensive. <P>Everyone,<P>I have considered this swap many times.  The main benefits are that you gain a rock solid (mini-mouse) block.  This is not a feature of the 60 degree six engines.  Last time that I checked.. the 3.8L (in any version) is a 90 degree engine.  Realize that this has powered such great cars as the Buick Grand National and the majority of GM's current line up.  The statement about 90 degree engines being a lesser variety is preposterous.  The 4.3L has both a Vortech and Paxton supercharger that are a direct fit as well.  The best thing is that this block can handle anything that you put in it.  Parts are available if you need to buy a sbc rebuild kit/pistons/rods it will probably be less than most 6's and readily available.  You could even use Syclone/Typhoon parts if your budget was high enough.  I still say go with the 4.3L.  I think that there are/were a few that had this powerplant on the fiero-list.  Check there... it can be done, it just requires slightly more work than a 3.4L, but you will have the justification of being able to blow the doors off of almost anything that calls you out.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000007||Roy||01-28-2000||01:21 AM||||Thanks everyone, depending where I am in the next few months money wise. I may consider doing that. I have the engine so thats not a problem. My 84 is in the garage with no engine in it at all, so I'm think. Why the hell not just give the 84 the power it diserves:P. I need to get rid of one of my fiero though. I bought one too many <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> I got carried away I guess, then realized I can't afford three of them. I'll most likely be selling my running 86.5 in a few weeks on here. I just gotta clean it up wax it. Give it a tune up..ect. My 88 is my baby though I couldn't sell her. ||||reg||10
Z||000008||batboy||01-28-2000||07:19 AM||||I totally agree with William this time. The 4.3 engine is a big displacement V6 that is powerful and very reliable. It is beefy and torquey. Lots of high performance parts for it. I personally think that the 3800 or the 4.3 is the best V6 swaps you can perform. Not the most economical, because they are not cheap to do, but the next best thing to a V8 without the extra weight.||||reg||1
